  <h2>Why I built it</h2>
  <p>
    YouTube Music doesn’t show you any listening stats. The usual play-tracking tools miss plays, and they can’t tell
    a song I finished from one I skipped five seconds in. I wanted a complete history that I own, plus a dashboard that shows what I listen to, when I listen, and which old favorites I’ve stopped playing.
  </p>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <p>
    I import my full history from Google Takeout once. After that, a scheduled job checks my recent history every
    20 minutes and figures out what’s new. The hard part is that YouTube Music removes duplicates from
    that list, so a song I replay jumps to the top and disappears from its old spot. The sync has to line the new
    list up with the last one while allowing for those moves.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every play is stored once, keyed on the track and the time. Synced plays only have estimated times, so when a
    newer Takeout export comes in, its exact rows replace the estimates for that period. Importing again never
    double counts.
  </p>

  <h2>Deciding what counts</h2>
  <p>
    Before I could measure listening time, I had to decide what counts as a play and what counts as a skip. I wrote those rules down first:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>A song counts as <strong>skipped</strong> if the next one starts before it’s halfway through (and within four minutes).</li>
    <li><strong>Listening time</strong> is the song length or the gap to the next song, whichever is shorter.</li>
    <li>A <strong>session</strong> ends after 30 minutes of silence.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Every chart on the dashboard follows those rules.</p>

  <h2>Making sure it’s right</h2>
  <p>
    Each awkward case has its own test: nothing new, new songs on top, a replayed song jumping up, the whole list
    changing, a first run, and a Takeout import replacing synced plays. The stats tests use tiny hand-built
    histories where I already know how many skips and sessions there should be.
  </p>

